<p>An <b>atrium</b> is the open central court of an ancient Roman house, lit from above through the compluvium, an opening in the roof. Rain falling through it was gathered in the impluvium, a shallow pool set into the floor, and led to a cistern below.</p>
<p>The rooms of the house opened onto the atrium: the tablinum, where the master of the house received clients, faced the entrance across it, and small rooms along its sides served for storage and sleeping.</p>
<p id="plan">The plan of a typical house ran from the street door through the fauces into the atrium, then the tablinum, and beyond it the peristyle garden.</p>
